  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how activation of the fatty-acid receptor FFAR4 can link anti-inflammatory effects in macrophages with improved insulin action in adipocytes. On the left, inflammatory stimuli such as LPS and TNF activate TLR4 and TNFR in a macrophage, converging on TAK1 and then NFκB/JNK signaling, which promotes cytokine production. These cytokines drive inflammation and contribute to insulin resistance. FFAR4, stimulated by omega-3 fatty acids or synthetic agonists, appears to interrupt this pathway through β-arrestin 2 and TAB1, thereby dampening TAK1-dependent inflammatory signaling.\n\nOn the right, the same receptor is shown in an adipocyte, where FFAR4 signaling feeds into a pathway involving Gαq/11 and PI3K. This pathway is presented alongside the classical insulin receptor–IRS1 route, with both converging on PI3K to promote glucose uptake. The overall message is that FFAR4 activation has a dual metabolic benefit: it suppresses macrophage-driven inflammatory signaling that can worsen insulin resistance, while also enhancing insulin-related glucose uptake in fat cells. Together, the schematic suggests a mechanism by which omega-3 fatty acids or FFAR4 agonists may improve metabolic health.",
